<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander I of Epirus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 370–331 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Alexander the Molossian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, called the Molossian, was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a> in Epirus and the brother-in-law and uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Invited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> to fight the Italic peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, he campaigned against the Lucanians, Bruttii and Samnites from 334 BCE. He was killed near Pandosia in 331 BCE, a model for his relative Pyrrhus's later expedition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus.jpg" alt="Alexander I of Epirus" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_the_Molossian_King_of_Epeiros_350_330_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
